<chapter>

<title><classname>HttpdUtilities</classname> Reference</title>
 <section><title>Introduction</title>
  
  <para>
   <classname>HttpdUtilities</classname> is a static class that is used
   to hold various helper routines that the Seminole core depends on.
  </para>
  
  <para>
   Most of these routines may be called by your handlers as well, 
   so it is important that they be well understood.
  </para>

 </section>

 <section><title>Public Methods</title>

  <section><title><function>StrVCat</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       char
       *<function><classname>HttpdUtilities</classname>::StrVCat</function>
      </funcdef>
  
      <paramdef>const char *<parameter>p_first</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>...</paramdef>
  
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    This routine will concatenate a <symbol>NULL</symbol> terminated list of
    strings and return a pointer to the resultant string in
    storage obtained from <function>malloc()</function>.
   </para>

   <para>
    It is important to terminate the list with 
    <parameter class=function>(char *)0</parameter>, not
    <symbol>NULL</symbol>, because some <hardware>CPU</hardware>
    architectures have different <symbol>NULL</symbol> pointer 
    representations for different types and the compiler does not know
    the type of the pointer because it is a variable argument list.
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title><function>StrChop</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       char
       *<function><classname>HttpdUtilities</classname>::StrChop</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>char *&amp;<parameter>p_string</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>
   
   <para>
    This routine will tokenize a white-space delimited string.  A
    pointer to the next token (within <parameter
    class=function>p_string</parameter>) is returned by the function.
    In addition, <parameter class=function>p_string</parameter> is
    updated to point to the next token so that successive calls to
    <function>StrChop</function> will tokenize an entire input string.
    An empty string is returned when no more tokens are available.
   </para>

  </section>
  <section><title><function>StrCmpi</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       int
       <function><classname>HttpdUtilities</classname>::StrCmpi</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>const char *<parameter>p_a</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>const char *<parameter>p_b</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>
   
   <para>
    Carry out a case-insensitive lexicographic comparison between
    <parameter class=function>p_a</parameter> and <parameter
    class=function>p_b</parameter>.  Returns
    <returnvalue>0</returnvalue> if they are equal, less than 0 if
    <parameter class=function>p_a</parameter> is lexicographically
    less than <parameter class=function>p_b</parameter>, or greater
    than 0 if <parameter class=function>p_a</parameter> is
    lexicographically greater than <parameter
    class=function>p_b</parameter>.
   </para>

  </section>
 
  <section><title><function>GetLcExtension</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       char
       *<function><classname>HttpdUtilities</classname>::GetLcExtension</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>char *<parameter>p_file_name</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    Get the extension of a file name (in lower case).
   </para>

   <para>
    The input string is modified in place and the return value points
    into that string. You should make a copy of the string for this
    routine if you need it after the extension is obtained.
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title><function>GetComponentPath</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       char
*<function><classname>HttpdUtilities</classname>::GetComponentPath</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>const char *<parameter>p_uri</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>const char
      *<parameter>p_filename</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>
   
   <para>
    Concatenate the two passed strings to form a single 
    path. Trailing forward slashes are adjusted so as to avoid 
    duplicates.
   </para>

  </section>

  <section id="utNormalize" xreflabel="HttpdUtilities::Normalize">
  <title><function>Normalize</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       char
*<function><classname>HttpdUtilities</classname>::Normalize</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>const char *<parameter>uri</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>const char
      *<parameter>prefix</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>
   
   <para>
    Prepends the string <parameter class=function>prefix</parameter>
    to the string <parameter class=function>uri</parameter> and
    removes any <token>.</token> or <token>..</token> references. It
    returns a pointer to the resultant string in storage obtained from
    <function>malloc()</function>. It is the caller's responsibility
    to free it (using <xref linkend="opsFree">).
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title><function>HasTrailingSlash</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       bool
       <function><classname>HttpdUtilities</classname>::HasTrailingSlash</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>const char *<parameter>p_path</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    This function returns <returnvalue>true</returnvalue> if
    <parameter class=function>p_path</parameter> ends in a forward
    slash.
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title><function>HasPrefix</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       bool
       <function><classname>HttpdUtilities</classname>::HasPrefix</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>const char *<parameter>p_str</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>const char *<parameter>p_prefix</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>
   
   <para>
    Determine if a string has a certain prefix (case insensitive).
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title><function>IsUriProtocol</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       bool
       <function><classname>HttpdUtilities</classname>::IsUriProtocol</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>const char *<parameter>p_uri</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    Determine if a <acronym>URI</acronym> contains a protocol. The
    currently supported protocols are:
    
    <itemizedlist>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       <parameter class=function>http:</parameter>
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       <parameter class=function>ftp:</parameter>
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       <parameter class=function>file:</parameter>
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </itemizedlist>
        
    This is useful for deciding if a <acronym>URL</acronym> is
    relative or absolute.
   
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title><function>UriEncode</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
char *<function><classname>HttpdUtilities</classname>::UriEncode</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>const char *<parameter>p_uri</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    <acronym>URL</acronym>-encode a string, being safe about what we
    quote.  Returns a pointer to an encoded string on success,
    <symbol>NULL</symbol> on failure.  It is the caller's
    responsibility to free it (using <xref linkend="opsFree">).
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title><function>UriDecode</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
char *<function><classname>HttpdUtilities</classname>::UriDecode</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>const char *<parameter>p_encoded</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    <acronym>URL</acronym>-decode a string, being safe about what we
    quote.  Returns a pointer to the decoded string on success,
    <symbol>NULL</symbol> on failure.  It is the caller's
    responsibility to free it (using <xref linkend="opsFree">).
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title><function>HtmlQuote</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
char *<function><classname>HttpdUtilities</classname>::HtmlQuote</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>const char *<parameter>p_str</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    Escape any <acronym>HTML</acronym> specific character entities in
    <parameter class=function>p_str</parameter>.  Returns a pointer to
    an encoded string on success, <symbol>NULL</symbol> on failure.
    It is the caller's responsibility to free it (using <xref
    linkend="opsFree">).
   </para>

  </section>

  <section id="utLookup" xreflabel="HttpdUtilities::Lookup">
  <title><function>Lookup</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
const char *<function><classname>HttpdUtilities</classname>::Lookup</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>const HttpdPair *<parameter>p_table</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>size_t<parameter> table_size</parameter></paramdef>     
      <paramdef>const char *<parameter>p_key</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>int (*p_comp)(const char *p_a, const char *p_b)</paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    Carries out a binary search for key <parameter
    class=function>p_key</parameter> in the pre-sorted table 
    <parameter class=function>p_table</parameter>, which contains
    <parameter class=function>table_size</parameter> elements.  If
    desired, the user-supplied comparison function <parameter
    class=function>p_comp</parameter> is used, otherwise
    <function>strcmp()</function> is called.
   </para>

   <para>
    Returns the discovered value upon success, <symbol>NULL</symbol>
    upon failure.
   </para>

  </section>

  <section id="utFormatTime" xreflabel="HttpdUtilities::FormatTime">
  <title><function>FormatTime</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
void<function><classname> HttpdUtilities</classname>::FormatTime</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>char *<parameter>p_buf</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>size_t<parameter> bufsz</parameter></paramdef>     
      <paramdef>const char *<parameter>p_format</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>time_t<parameter> t</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    Formats <acronym>ANSI</acronym> C <type>time_t</type> value
    <parameter class=function>t</parameter> according to the specification
    supplied in <parameter class=function>p_format</parameter>, and
    stores the result in <parameter class=function>p_buf</parameter>
    (previously allocated by the caller to be <parameter
    class=function>bufsz</parameter> in length.)
   </para>

   <para>
    For more information, please refer to your C library documentation
    on the standard <function>strftime()</function> function.
   </para>

  </section>

 </section>

 <section><title>Public Data</title>

  <section><title><varname>mRoot</varname></title>
   <para>
    <literal>const char</literal><varname>
    mRoot[]</varname><token>;</token>
   </para>

   <para>
    This variable represents the path separator used in concatenating
    path segments, and other miscellaneous uses.  Its default value of
    <quote>/</quote> should not be changed without considerable
    thought, since <acronym>URI</acronym> formatting standards specify 
    it as a hierarchical separator.
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title><varname>mNetTimeFormat</varname></title>
   <para>
    <literal>const char</literal><varname> mNetTimeFormat[]</varname>
    <token>;</token>
   </para>

   <para>
    Supplied as a parameter to <xref linkend="utFormatTime">,
    this is a string representing the desired human-readable format to
    which system time should be converted.  In an
    <acronym>ANSI</acronym> C environment, the underlying library
    function ultimately responsible for this transformation is
    <function>strftime()</function>.  For further information on the
    contents of this variable, local C library documentation on that
    function should be consulted.
   </para>

  </section>

 </section>
</chapter>

